% -e over a rule whose second demand call restates its head: p(a, Y)? adorns
% p with its first column bound, and the call p(X, Z), made after q(X) has
% bound X, reads p's bf adornment with the head's own bound argument.  Its
% magic rule would derive only the guard's tuple, so it has none, and no cut
% is made before it: the adorned rule reads the guard and q's call itself,
% and no component is recursive through the magic predicate.

r(a).
r(b).
s(a, b).
s(b, c).

q(X) :- r(X).

p(a, a).
p(X, Y) :- q(X), p(X, Z), s(Z, Y).

p(a, Y)?
